I now have everything I need. Here is the full, evidenced report.

---

## Findings

### 1. Tool Confirmation Mechanism in `ToolDefinition` / `registerTool`

There is NO built-in confirmation/approval field in `ToolDefinition`. The interface at `/Users/luke/Projects/personal/pi-mono-fork/packages/coding-agent/src/core/extensions/types.ts:726-791` defines exactly these optional fields: `name`, `label`, `description`, `promptSnippet`, `promptGuidelines`, `deferLoading`, `alwaysLoad`, `replacesBuiltins`, `searchHint`, `providers`, `parameters`, `renderShell`, `prepareArguments`, `executionMode`, `execute`, `renderCall`, `renderResult`. No `confirm`, `confirmation`, `needsConfirmation`, `permission`, `approval`, `requiresApproval`, or `getConfirmation` field exists.

The `registerTool` signature (types.ts:1494) accepts only `ToolDefinition<TParams, TDetails, TState>` with no additional options object. There is no approval callback or permission gate at the registration layer.

There is a separate `tool_call` event hook (`pi.on("tool_call", handler)`) that can return `{ block: true, reason: string }` (types.ts:1344-1348, 1484). This fires before execute, but it receives the event from outside the tool's own code — it is a session-level interceptor, not a field on `ToolDefinition`.

---

### 2. What `ctx` Is Inside `execute`, and Whether `ctx.ui` Is Available There

The 5th argument `ctx` in `execute(toolCallId, params, signal, onUpdate, ctx)` is typed as `ExtensionContext` (types.ts:773-779). The full `ExtensionContext` interface (types.ts:559-627) includes:

```ts
ui: ExtensionUIContext;   // types.ts:561
hasUI: boolean;           // types.ts:563
```

`ExtensionUIContext` (types.ts:221-372) exposes:
- `select(title: string, options: string[], opts?: ExtensionUIDialogOptions): Promise<string | undefined>` (types.ts:223)
- `confirm(title: string, message: string, opts?: ExtensionUIDialogOptions): Promise<boolean>` (types.ts:226)
- `input(title: string, placeholder?: string, opts?: ExtensionUIDialogOptions): Promise<string | undefined>` (types.ts:229)
- `notify(message: string, type?: "info" | "warning" | "error"): void` (types.ts:232)

**These are available inside `execute`.** The proof is `pi-mode`'s `ExitPlanMode` tool:

`/Users/luke/Projects/personal/my-pi/extensions/pi-mode/src/index.ts:128-150`:
```ts
async execute(_id, params, _signal, _onUpdate, ctx) {
    if (!ctx.hasUI) {
        // headless fallback
        return { content: [...] };
    }
    const choice = await ctx.ui.select(title, [EXIT_APPROVE, EXIT_KEEP]);
    if (choice === EXIT_APPROVE) { ... }
    return { content: [...] };
}
```

This is a registered tool (`pi.registerTool`) calling `ctx.ui.select(...)` directly inside its `execute` handler, exactly the pattern you want. The `ctx.ui.confirm(...)` method exists on the same interface and is used from command handlers (`pi-goal/src/commands.ts:107-110`, `pi-routine/src/dispatch/catchup-run.ts:130`, `pi-routine/src/dispatch/catchup-run.ts:152`).

The `ExtensionCommandContext` (types.ts:633-664) is a superset of `ExtensionContext` — commands get extras like `waitForIdle`, `newSession`, `fork`. Tool `execute` gets the base `ExtensionContext`, which still includes full `ui`. So `ctx.ui.select(...)` and `ctx.ui.confirm(...)` are available mid-tool-call.

---

### 3. How Other Extensions Gate Expensive/Destructive Actions

Three distinct patterns found:

**Pattern A — `ctx.ui.select(...)` inside `execute` as the gate (pi-mode ExitPlanMode)**

`/Users/luke/Projects/personal/my-pi/extensions/pi-mode/src/index.ts:128-150`

The model calls a tool; the tool's `execute` blocks on `ctx.ui.select(...)`. The user's choice determines whether to proceed or return a "not approved" message to the model.

**Pattern B — `ctx.ui.confirm(...)` in a command handler before starting an action**

`/Users/luke/Projects/personal/my-pi/extensions/pi-goal/src/commands.ts:103-114`:
```ts
if (!ctx.hasUI) {
    ctx.ui.notify("Clear the existing goal before replacing it.", "error");
    return;
}
const shouldReplace = await ctx.ui.confirm(
    "Replace goal?",
